 410:./ucos/Core/os_task.c **** /*
 411:./ucos/Core/os_task.c **** ***************************************************************************************************
 412:./ucos/Core/os_task.c **** *                                            DELETE A TASK
 413:./ucos/Core/os_task.c **** *
 414:./ucos/Core/os_task.c **** * Description: This function allows you to delete a task.  The calling task can delete itself by
 415:./ucos/Core/os_task.c **** *              its own priority number.  The deleted task is returned to the dormant state and can 
 416:./ucos/Core/os_task.c **** *              re-activated by creating the deleted task again.
 417:./ucos/Core/os_task.c **** *
 418:./ucos/Core/os_task.c **** * Arguments  : prio    is the priority of the task to delete.  Note that you can explicitly delete
 419:./ucos/Core/os_task.c **** *                      the current task without knowing its priority level by setting 'prio' to
 420:./ucos/Core/os_task.c **** *                      OS_PRIO_SELF.
 421:./ucos/Core/os_task.c **** *
 422:./ucos/Core/os_task.c **** * Returns    : OS_ERR_NONE                  if the call is successful
 423:./ucos/Core/os_task.c **** *              OS_ERR_ILLEGAL_DEL_RUN_TIME  if you tried to delete a task after safety critical ope
 424:./ucos/Core/os_task.c **** *                                           started.
 425:./ucos/Core/os_task.c **** *              OS_ERR_TASK_DEL_IDLE         if you attempted to delete uC/OS-II's idle task
 426:./ucos/Core/os_task.c **** *              OS_ERR_PRIO_INVALID          if the priority you specify is higher that the maximum 
 427:./ucos/Core/os_task.c **** *                                           (i.e. >= OS_LOWEST_PRIO) or, you have not specified OS_
 428:./ucos/Core/os_task.c **** *              OS_ERR_TASK_DEL              if the task is assigned to a Mutex PIP.
 429:./ucos/Core/os_task.c **** *              OS_ERR_TASK_NOT_EXIST        if the task you want to delete does not exist.
 430:./ucos/Core/os_task.c **** *              OS_ERR_TASK_DEL_ISR          if you tried to delete a task from an ISR
 431:./ucos/Core/os_task.c **** *
 432:./ucos/Core/os_task.c **** * Notes      : 1) To reduce interrupt latency, OSTaskDel() 'disables' the task:
 433:./ucos/Core/os_task.c **** *                    a) by making it not ready
 434:./ucos/Core/os_task.c **** *                    b) by removing it from any wait lists
 435:./ucos/Core/os_task.c **** *                    c) by preventing OSTimeTick() from making the task ready to run.
 436:./ucos/Core/os_task.c **** *                 The task can then be 'unlinked' from the miscellaneous structures in uC/OS-II.
 437:./ucos/Core/os_task.c **** *              2) The function OS_Dummy() is called after OS_EXIT_CRITICAL() because, on most proce
 438:./ucos/Core/os_task.c **** *                 the next instruction following the enable interrupt instruction is ignored.
 439:./ucos/Core/os_task.c **** *              3) An ISR cannot delete a task.
 440:./ucos/Core/os_task.c **** *              4) The lock nesting counter is incremented because, for a brief instant, if the curr
 441:./ucos/Core/os_task.c **** *                 task is being deleted, the current task would not be able to be rescheduled becau
 442:./ucos/Core/os_task.c **** *                 is removed from the ready list.  Incrementing the nesting counter prevents anothe
 443:./ucos/Core/os_task.c **** *                 from being schedule.  This means that an ISR would return to the current task whi
 444:./ucos/Core/os_task.c **** *                 being deleted.  The rest of the deletion would thus be able to be completed.
 445:./ucos/Core/os_task.c **** ***************************************************************************************************
 446:./ucos/Core/os_task.c **** */
 447:./ucos/Core/os_task.c **** 
